# PLC-Based Conveyor Jam Detection and Sequencing

## Project Overview
A PLC-based conveyor control system developed in EDWartens Lab using Ladder Logic. The system models three sequential conveyor belts that transport parcels toward a bagging area, with timed startup, photoelectric jam detection, fault latching, and operator reset.

## Problem Statement
Conveyor jams can interrupt parcel handling and damage equipment if upstream belts continue feeding a blocked conveyor. The system must detect prolonged sensor blockage, stop affected upstream conveyors, and alert the operator.

## Key Features
- **Sequential startup:** CV_3 starts first, followed by CV_2 and CV_1 with 800 ms delays.
- **Jam detection:** TON timers detect continuous photo-eye blockage for 5 seconds while the corresponding conveyor runs.
- **Fault latching:** Individual jam flags retain the fault until reset.
- **Upstream interlocking:** A downstream jam is intended to stop the affected conveyor and all upstream conveyors.
- **Alarm indication:** A common alarm activates when any jam flag is set.
- **Operator controls:** Start, Stop, Emergency Stop, and Reset inputs.

## Tools
- EDWartens Lab
- Ladder Logic
- PLC simulation
- TON timers and digital I/O

## Simulation Status
The program compiled without errors, and basic conveyor startup and stop/restart behavior were observed in simulation. Jam detection, reset interlocks, and emergency-stop behavior still require complete validation.

## Safety Note
This is an educational simulation. A real machine emergency stop requires appropriately designed and validated safety hardware; ordinary PLC logic alone is not a safety-rated emergency-stop system.

## Future Improvements
- Complete all jam and reset functional tests.
- Verify startup timing and downstream/upstream stop behavior.
- Capture simulation evidence and document test results.
